body:
For now, the `pre/post` link hooks are plugin specific. That means a plugin can specify an arbitrary code to run after its being linked (`postlink/prelink`). 

What we want is the ability to define it on a project level, so that it gets executed anytime `link` is run.

Setup:
```json
{
  "rnpm": {
     "commands": {
       "postlink": ""
    }
  }
}
```
which is exactly what a plugin can specify.

A simple use-case for that is you want to perform a custom tweak to your `AppDelegate.m` every time new dependency is added.

CC: @brentvatne @satya164

Can we replace:
```
maven {
            // All of React Native (JS, Obj-C sources, Android binaries) is installed from npm
            url("$rootDir/../../../node_modules/react-native/android")
        }
        maven {
            // Android JSC is installed from npm
            url("$rootDir/../../../node_modules/jsc-android/dist")
        }

```
with a CLI-generated code? CLI is able to detect the path to `react-native` dynamically, would require fewer changes to be applied by users when dealing with a `mono repo`.

It would be great if we could also replace:
```
apply from: "../../../../node_modules/react-native/react.gradle"
```
with
```
apply from: {dynamicallyResolvedReactNativePath}
```
(e.g. previously set by the CLI global variable).

I am opening this issue to track work required to offer first-class support for CocoaPods in `link` and `unlink` commands.

@fson is [working on improving general CocoaPods support](https://github.com/react-native-community/discussions-and-proposals/issues/96) in React Native and making it the integral part of every new app inited with `react-native init`.

We should make sure that CLI work is done and released before this PR gets merged.

Please provide all the information requested. Issues that do not follow this format are likely to stall.

## Description
After upgrading from 0.61.4 to 0.62.0 the app will not upload files anymore from Android (all other requests are working fine)

## React Native version:
0.62.0

## Steps To Reproduce
1. Install a fresh ReactNative app via CLI
2. Upload a file to the emulator
3. Try to upload a file using `fetch`

```

import Picker from "react-native-image-picker"
import {request, PERMISSIONS, RESULTS} from 'react-native-permissions';

class App extends React.Component {

  async componentDidMount() {

    try {
      await request(PERMISSIONS.ANDROID.WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE)
      await Picker.launchImageLibrary({noData: true}, async (file) => {
        try {
           const body = new FormData()
           body.append("file", { type: file.type, size: file.fileSize, uri: `file://${file.path}`, name: file.fileName })
           const headers = { "content-type": "multipart/form-data", "accept": "application/json" }

          const req = await fetch("ANY_SERVER/upload/image", {
            method: "POST",
            headers,
            body
          })
          console.log(req.status)
        }  catch (e) {
          console.log(e)
        }
      })
    } catch (e) {
      console.log(e)
    }
  }

  render(){
    return <View/>
  }
}
```

## Expected Results
The request should reach the server to upload the file

